      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How many tablespoons in 1/3 cup?</h3>
        <p><strong>5 tablespoons + 1 teaspoon</strong> = ⅓ cup (5.333 tbsp exactly). Since you can't measure a third of a tablespoon cleanly, recipes use "5 tbsp + 1 tsp."</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How many tablespoons in 1/2 cup?</h3>
        <p><strong>8 tablespoons</strong> = ½ cup. Half of 16 = 8. Easy to remember.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How many tablespoons in 2/3 cup?</h3>
        <p><strong>10 tablespoons + 2 teaspoons</strong> = ⅔ cup (10.667 tbsp exactly). Common in baking recipes for butter or sugar.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How many tablespoons in 3/4 cup?</h3>
        <p><strong>12 tablespoons</strong> = ¾ cup. Three-quarters of 16 = 12 exactly.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How do I convert tablespoons back to cups?</h3>
        <p>Divide by 16. Example: 24 tablespoons ÷ 16 = <strong>1.5 cups</strong> (1½ cups). Use the "Tbsp → Cups" mode in the calculator above.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How many tablespoons is half a cup of butter?</h3>
        <p>Half a cup of butter = <strong>8 tablespoons</strong> = 1 standard stick of butter (in the US). Butter sticks are marked with tablespoon lines on the wrapper for easy measuring.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>How many teaspoons are in a tablespoon?</h3>
        <p>There are <strong>3 teaspoons in 1 tablespoon</strong>. So 1 cup = 16 tablespoons = 48 teaspoons. If a recipe says 1 tablespoon and you only have a teaspoon, use 3 teaspoons.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <h3>Is a tablespoon the same in US and metric recipes?</h3>
        <p>Not exactly. A US tablespoon = 14.787 ml, while a metric tablespoon (Australia, UK, Canada) = 15 ml. The difference is tiny (~1.4%) and won't affect most recipes, but matters for precise baking.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

